    "q-342240": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342240",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $z>0,$ simplify $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})$ using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{6}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$",
      "worked_solution": "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}+{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2})^{3}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2})^{-3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{-6})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{6}+{z}^{-6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
